C = [
("h1", "17. Objections and Replies"),
("p", "This chapter takes the strongest objections to the position and answers each from Scripture. The objections are stated as their holders state them. Three modern writers are named, each from a free full text the author has read: Thomas L. Constable, in his notes on 1 Corinthians, Ephesians, 1 Timothy and Titus; John Piper, in his answer on 1 Timothy 2:12 for the Ask Pastor John podcast; and N. T. Wright, in his 2004 paper on women’s service in the church. Each is quoted only from those texts. Each agrees with the volume at some points and differs at others, and the chapter says which is which. Where an objection is held by a tradition and none of the three was read on it, it is stated in the volume’s words, and no sentence is placed in any person’s mouth."),
("sub", "17.1 Objection: pastor, elder and overseer are one office"),
("p", "Constable holds that the overseer “is evidently synonymous with ‘elder’” and also with “pastor,” and Piper writes that “those are the same office in Paul’s mind.” On this reading the “pastors and teachers” of Ephesians 4:11 are the elders of the local church, and the pastor’s office is an office in Paul."),
("p", "The volume agrees that elder and overseer are one: Titus 1:5 and 1:7 say so in one argument. It does not add “pastor,” and the reason is the text. The word is found in Paul in one verse, and the verse does not call the pastors elders. It lists them among the gifts the ascended Christ gave, with apostles, prophets and evangelists. Constable himself sets two readings of the clause side by side and says that the Greek “may describe two types of gifted people.” The volume reads the passage as a list of gifts and does not turn it into the name of an office. It does not deny that the elders “feed the church of God” (Acts 20:28) as shepherds do. It notes that Paul gives that work to the overseers by another verse and does not give the title."),
("sub", "17.2 Objection: the head covering is only custom"),
("p", "Constable concludes of 1 Corinthians 11 that Paul “appealed to his readers to understand God’s intention for men and women, and to maintain a custom, not to obey a command of God.” Piper writes that “The Bible is very aware of the role of culture in shaping the symbols of man’s headship and woman’s submission.” On this reading the covering is a custom of one culture, and what lasts is the headship and creation behind it."),
("p", "The volume agrees with the distinction and does not draw the conclusion that the covering is only custom. Paul’s grounds are of different kinds, as chapter 10 sets out: the order of heads and creation are stated as facts, the angels are named without explanation, and nature and the churches’ practice are appeals to propriety. A reading that treats all five as custom does not account for the first two, which are not about Corinth. A reading that treats all five as binding the covering as a rule does not account for the appeals to judgment. The volume reports each ground and does not decide the covering. It agrees with both writers that what lasts is what Paul grounds on creation."),
("sub", "17.3 Objection: 1 Corinthians 14:34–35 is about order, or is not Paul’s"),
("p", "Wright is “in two minds whether to agree with those who say this verse is a later and non-Pauline interpolation,” and he finds the strongest explanation in Ken Bailey’s reading: women who did not follow the language of the service talked among themselves, and were told to be quiet. “This would fit extremely well,” he writes, with Paul’s concern for order. Constable records that Barrett and Fee hold the verses to be added, and he reports the common view as “that Paul forbade some form of inappropriate speech, but not all speech.”"),
("p", "The volume agrees with Constable’s common view and with Wright that the verses cannot mean that women had no part in the meeting. It answers the objection on two points. The ground Paul states is not noise but “obedience, as also saith the law” (1 Corinthians 14:34), and a reading that makes disorder the ground does not state Paul’s ground. And the text question does not touch the argument, since the volume reads the verses as the KJV has them and rests nothing on them alone. The instruction of 1 Timothy 2:11–14 says the same of the church in its own letter."),
("sub", "17.4 Objection: 1 Timothy 2:12 does not forbid women to teach"),
("p", "Wright reads verse 12 as a disclaimer: “I don’t mean to imply that I’m now setting up women as the new authority over men in the same way that previously men held authority over women.” He grounds it in the cult of Artemis at Ephesus, and he reads verse 11 as a command that “women, too, should be allowed to study and learn.” The volume agrees with the second point, and notes that it is a large one: the woman is to learn. It answers the first from the next verse. Verse 13 begins with “For,” and gives “Adam was first formed, then Eve” as the reason. A disclaimer would not need a reason from Genesis. The reasons of verses 13 and 14 are given for a prohibition, and Piper’s statement of the ground applies: Paul’s “support was not to argue from culture but from creation.”"),
("sub", "17.5 Objection: the deception in 1 Timothy 2:14 is local to Ephesus"),
("p", "Some commentaries hold that Paul’s mention of the deception of Eve points to women in the Ephesian church who had been led astray, and that the instruction is therefore bound to that situation. No writer is named here, because none of the three was read as holding it, and the objection is stated in the volume’s words. The volume answers that Paul gives two reasons, not one. The first, “Adam was first formed, then Eve,” is not about any deception, and it is not about Ephesus. If the second were local, the first would stand. And Paul states both as the reason for the instruction. He does not say that the instruction is for the women who were deceived. The volume does not deny that false teachers had misled some in Ephesus; it observes that Paul does not give that as his reason."),
("sub", "17.6 Objection: Galatians 3:28 abolishes the distinction"),
("p", "Wright notes that Galatians 3:28, “there is neither male nor female: for ye are all one in Christ Jesus,” has been used as a rallying cry by those who read the instruction on women as outdated. He does not use it so. He writes that “The difference is irrelevant for membership status and membership badges. But it is still to be taken note of when it comes to pastoral practice,” and that “We do not become hermaphrodites or for that matter genderless, sexless beings when we are baptised.” The volume agrees with him on the point that matters. Galatians 3:26–28 says that all are children of God by faith in Christ Jesus and that standing in Christ is one. It does not say that every instruction about the order of the assembly is removed. The same Paul wrote Galatians and 1 Corinthians, and the grounds he gives in the second are not set aside by the standing he gives in the first."),
("p", "The volume does not adopt Wright’s reading of Galatians 3 as the renewed family of Abraham. It leaves Galatians 3 to the volume on the seed, under the standing sentence: the seed is prophetic fulfillment in Christ; the Body receives blessing through Christ alone, not through participation in the Abrahamic covenant. What the volume needs from the verse is only that standing in Christ is stated, and that the same letters that state it give the instructions this volume reads."),
("sub", "17.7 Objection: the pastoral letters reflect a later church order"),
("p", "A common objection holds that the elders and deacons of 1 Timothy and Titus belong to a church order later than Paul’s. It is stated here in the volume’s words, and no writer is named. Wright says that he sets aside the question of who wrote 1 Timothy and does not discount the letter for being different. The volume replies from the undisputed letters. Philippians 1:1 names “the bishops and deacons.” 1 Corinthians 12:28 lists “governments.” Romans 12:8 has “he that ruleth.” 1 Thessalonians 5:12 speaks of those “over you in the Lord.” Paul names leaders, and the offices of bishop and deacon, in letters that no one assigns to a later hand. The volume reads the letters to Timothy and Titus as they present themselves, “Paul, an apostle of Jesus Christ by the commandment of God our Saviour” (1 Timothy 1:1)."),
("sub", "17.8 Objection: Piper presses further than the text"),
("p", "Piper draws a wider conclusion from 1 Timothy 2:12. “Spiritually mature womanhood should disincline a woman from taking opportunities to teach men (including mixed adult groups) wherever that role would embody the kind of authoritative engagement that would be like the teaching and authority of an elder in the church.” Constable draws a narrower one: Paul “cited God’s intention for male/female relationships specifically in a marriage context, not in general social situations,” and the church is the household of God."),
("p", "The volume stops where the passage stops. Paul’s instruction is about the church, “the house of God” (1 Timothy 3:15), and about the teaching and authority the overseer exercises there. It does not say what a woman may do in a classroom, a mixed group or a Bible study held outside the church’s gathering. The volume notes that Paul himself has older women teach younger women (Titus 2:3, 4) and that Timothy learned from his mother and grandmother (2 Timothy 1:5). Piper’s principle may be defended on other grounds. This volume does not claim it for Paul."),
("ctbl", "The objections at a glance.", [
    ("Objection", "Who holds it, as read", "The reply in one line", "Where answered"),
    ("Pastor, elder and overseer are one office", "Constable and Piper", "Elder and overseer are one in Titus 1; “pastors” is a gift in Ephesians 4:11", "17.1; chapters 5, 6"),
    ("The covering is only custom", "Constable; Piper on symbols", "Two grounds are creation and order; the rest are appeals to propriety", "17.2; chapter 10"),
    ("1 Corinthians 14:34 is about order or not Paul’s", "Wright, from Bailey; Barrett and Fee as Constable records", "The stated ground is obedience and the law, not noise", "17.3; chapter 11"),
    ("1 Timothy 2:12 is a disclaimer", "Wright", "The “For” of verse 13 gives a reason for a prohibition", "17.4; chapter 12"),
    ("The deception in 1 Timothy 2:14 is local", "A tradition, none named", "Creation, the first reason, is not about Ephesus", "17.5; chapter 12"),
    ("Galatians 3:28 abolishes the distinction", "A tradition; Wright disagrees with its use", "Standing in Christ is one thing; the order of the assembly is another", "17.6"),
    ("The pastoral letters are a later order", "A tradition, none named", "Philippians, Romans and 1 Corinthians name the same offices", "17.7; chapters 5, 6"),
    ("Piper presses further than the text", "Piper; Constable limits", "Paul’s instruction is about the church", "17.8; chapter 12"),
  ], [0.26, 0.22, 0.34, 0.18]),
]
